The population of the republic rose from 3,452,819 in 1736 to 4,851,270 in 1770, an increase of 40.5 per cent over thirty-four years.

The Zong class remained the largest, falling from 56.7 to 50.5 per cent of the population as the other classes grew faster. The Hachinawa rose from 28.5 to 31.7 per cent and the Mahik from 13.8 to 14.1.

The Other class, one per cent of the population in 1736, rose to 3.7 per cent by 1770. It grew slowly until 1760 and then more than doubled in the following decade, the [[Reinforcement of Freedom Act]] of 1761 having ended the return of escaped people to the colonies they had come from.

The eastern provinces grew fastest. Kayahachin rose from 718,957 to 1,318,643 and Tiengo from 487,231 to 835,836, against a national rate of 1.01 per cent a year. The western provinces grew at between 0.2 and 0.55 per cent.

